WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.898
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.530
RETO MEIER: Welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.970
We are here at the Dev Zone at
the Pizza Authenticator app.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.750
Terry, tell me, what
am I looking at here?

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.370
I was promised pie.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.660
All I see is pizza.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.820
What's going on?

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.720
TERRY RYAN: It is a
form of pie, right?

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.570
So we have an app here that
will authenticate pizza

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.320
to a certain region
of the country, right?

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.690
Like, we have New York
pizza, California pizza,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.710
Chicago pizza.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.990
This app will
judge how authentic

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.140
it is for what style
it's supposed to be.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.360
RETO MEIER: OK, interesting.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.970
So why don't you--

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.050
actually, why don't you
show us how it works?

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.140
TERRY RYAN: So we
have our pizza here,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.323
and we have our tablet here.

00:00:44.323 --> 00:00:45.240
RETO MEIER: Fantastic.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.730
TERRY RYAN: Make sure
it's in focus, and bam.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.580
And now Pizza Authenticator
is judging whether or not

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.340
this pizza is authentic.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.590
So it's chewing on it.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.040
And we see that it is
pretty confident that this

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.200
is New York style pizza.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.585
RETO MEIER: So definitely
pizza, probably from New York.

00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:00.210
TERRY RYAN: OK.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.168
RETO MEIER: OK, let's
check out this over here.

00:01:02.168 --> 00:01:04.780
That's a good looking pie.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.460
TERRY RYAN: This
is more Midwest.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.110
So let's see.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.980
Make sure it's in focus,
and we'll do it again.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.470
She's taking a bite out of that.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.450
Chicago style pizza.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:18.270
RETO MEIER: Chicago style--

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:18.530
all right.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:19.830
TERRY RYAN: That's good, right?

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.790
Now we'll finish up, and
we'll do this last one.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.650
RETO MEIER: Yeah,
let's do the last one,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.292
because I'm curious to what
style pizza this thinks it is.

00:01:25.292 --> 00:01:26.500
TERRY RYAN: I think it knows.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.707
I think it knows what it is.

00:01:27.707 --> 00:01:29.790
RETO MEIER: I can't think
of any pie that deserves

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.960
to have zucchini on it.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.040
TERRY RYAN: Yep.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.490
Well, here in the
wonderful Golden State,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.630
we make abominations.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.583
Yes, California-- yes, so it
knew it was California pizza.

00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:40.500
RETO MEIER: Excellent.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.625
Well, I mean, I'm a huge
supporter of anything that

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:44.820
involves this amount of pizza.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.183
But tell me, what is
the point of this?

00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:48.600
Like, why is this
in the Dev Zone?

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.280
Why have we got this?

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:49.570
Yeah.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.737
TERRY RYAN: So this uses
under-the-covers ML, right,

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:52.590
machine learning.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.540
And ML is really daunting.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.040
A lot of people
come to it, and they

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.130
think they need to be, like,
a data scientist to do it.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.470
And your rank-and-file
developer sometimes, you know,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.860
can't approach it.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.610
Well, we took kind of an
incredibly trivial use of ML

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.360
and trained it to
determine whether or not

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.009
pizza was authentic.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.530
And all of this we did.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.940
We grabbed the data, we
classified all the images.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.918
It was all done by me, who is an
app dev, not a data scientist.

00:02:18.918 --> 00:02:19.960
RETO MEIER: Right, right.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.170
TERRY RYAN: So it really
shows that anyone can do ML.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.063
If I can do it,
anyone can do it.

00:02:24.063 --> 00:02:26.480
RETO MEIER: So you could take
the same sort of technology,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.897
the same approach
here, and really

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:29.880
apply it to much more
meaningful projects.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.550
TERRY RYAN: Right, if you wanted
to take pictures, satellite

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.860
pictures, of forests and see
how they change over time

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.510
and see-- you could
identify trees

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.510
from the forest for
the trees, right?

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.800
You could do that with this.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.340
And that's a much more
serious application of this.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.412
But it's the same principle--
pizza on to forests.

00:02:47.412 --> 00:02:48.370
RETO MEIER: Absolutely.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.480
No, and it's a fun way to
approach machine learning,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.290
that sort of stuff.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.190
Fantastic.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

